### Description
Two resource methods in the same class whose paths diverge at a segment that is not a singleton string should be a compile-time error. nutcracker accepts them. Examples: `get users/[int id]` with `get users/[string name]`, and `get items/[int id]` with `get items/[int... ids]`. Exact duplicate paths are already rejected ("duplicate resource method"); only the divergence check is missing. The same happens in a `service class`.

### Steps to Reproduce
Run `go run ./cli/cmd run repro.bal` with:

```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

client class C {
    resource function get users/[int id]() returns int {
        return id;
    }

    resource function get users/[string name]() returns int { // @error diverges from users/[int] at a non-singleton segment
        return name.length();
    }

    resource function get items/[int id]() returns int {
        return id;
    }

    resource function get items/[int... ids]() returns int { // @error diverges from items/[int] (member vs rest)
        return ids.length();
    }
}

public function main() {
    C c = new;
    int a = c->/users/["bob"];
    int b = c->/items/[1]/[2];
    io:println(a + b);
}
```

**Actual output**
```
5
```

**Expected**
Compile errors on line 8 and line 16 (the second declaration of each conflicting pair).

**Spec reasoning**
- §5.6.3.5: "Two resource-method-decls X and Y that map to tuple type descriptors [X1,...,Xm] and [Y1,...,Yn] diverge at position i if and only if: i ≤ m; i ≤ n; the intersection of the sets of resource method names applicable for X and Y at position i is non-empty; for all j < i, Xj is the same type as Yj; Xi and Yi are not the same (to be the same, they must both be a member-type-descriptor or both be a tuple-rest-descriptor, and the types must be the same)."
- "If two resource-method-decls diverge at position i, then it is a compile-time error unless the resource-path of at least one of them maps to a tuple type descriptor that at position i has a member-type-descriptor that denotes a singleton string."
- `["users", int]` vs `["users", string]` diverge at position 2 (int vs string). `["items", int]` vs `["items", int...]` diverge at position 2 (member vs rest). Neither pair has a singleton string there.
- §6.8.1.4: resource-method-defn extends the semantics of resource-method-decl, so the rule applies to classes.
- The subset10 restriction on dispatch when more than one method matches does not apply: each call in the reproducer matches exactly one method, and this is a declaration-time check.

